<h1>Incident Response</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Gate can assemble guidance and local artifacts. It does not rotate a credential, investigate access, clean the source, or rewrite history for you.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Rotate first at the provider.</strong> Once a credential has been committed or pushed, moving it to <code>.env</code>, deleting a line, or rewriting Git history does not revoke copies that were already exposed.</div>

<h2 id="command">Command scope</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Staged files by default</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate incident</span>

<span class="cmt"># All tracked files</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate incident --all</span>

<span class="cmt"># Explicit target</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate incident path/to/file.js</span></div>

<p>The command scans the selected current files, assesses each finding against local Git state, and starts the workflow only for findings labelled <code>PUSHED</code>. If nothing is staged, no staged incident assessment occurs. It does not scan all history unless you separately use <code>gate scan --history N</code> or <code>gate purge</code>.</p>

<h2 id="exposure">Exposure labels</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Current basis</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>LOCAL</code></td><td>The literal secret was not found by local <code>git log -S</code>, or the file-level fallback has no history.</td><td>Does not rule out exposure through untracked copies, logs, caches, other branches/remotes, or services.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>COMMITTED</code></td><td>The literal was found in local history but not in local remote-tracking history.</td><td>Remote-tracking refs can be stale or incomplete.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>PUSHED</code></td><td>The literal was found under local remote-tracking refs, or the file-level fallback found the file there.</td><td>Means “seen in local knowledge of a remote ref,” not necessarily public, currently reachable, or still valid.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>UNKNOWN</code></td><td>Git state could not be assessed.</td><td>The incident command currently does not start its guided workflow for unknown findings.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>For long values and missing secret material, Gate falls back to file-level history. In that case a file's presence can produce a medium-confidence <code>PUSHED</code> label even when that particular secret was never pushed. The displayed “first pushed” time is currently based on when the file was added to a remote-tracking history, not necessarily when the secret first appeared.</p>

<h2 id="five-steps">What the five printed steps do</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Rotate:</strong> prints stored provider-specific web guidance and, for some providers, example CLI commands. It may check whether a provider CLI is installed. It does not run the rotation command.</li>
  <li><strong>Audit:</strong> prints a dashboard URL and suggested log-review text. It does not access or analyze provider audit logs.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean code:</strong> tells you to run <code>gate fix --file &lt;path&gt;</code>. It does not call the fixer from the incident workflow.</li>
  <li><strong>Scrub history:</strong> writes a reviewable shell script and plaintext replacement file under <code>~/.gate/purge/&lt;timestamp&gt;/</code>. It does not execute the script or push rewritten history.</li>
  <li><strong>Document:</strong> writes a JSON record under <code>~/.gate/incidents/</code>.</li>
</ol>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>“Workflow complete” means artifact generation finished.</strong> The CLI prints that phrase after the five sections, but the response actions are still manual. Provider instructions and example commands can become stale; confirm them in current provider documentation before acting.</div>

<h2 id="records">Incident records</h2>
<p>Each pushed finding gets a record named like <code>~/.gate/incidents/gate-inc-YYYYMMDD-001.json</code>. It includes rule, file, line, a coarse exposure window, static compliance references, and this initial action state:</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="str">"actions"</span>: {
  <span class="str">"rotated"</span>: <span class="val">false</span>,
  <span class="str">"accessLogsReviewed"</span>: <span class="val">false</span>,
  <span class="str">"codeFixed"</span>: <span class="val">false</span>,
  <span class="str">"historyPurged"</span>: <span class="str">"pending"</span>
}</div>
<p>The current workflow never updates these fields. If you edit a record manually, preserve it as evidence and document who made the change; Gate provides no signed workflow or approval log.</p>

<h2 id="report">Generate a Markdown incident report</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate incident report gate-inc-YYYYMMDD-001</span>
<span class="dim">gate-incident-gate-inc-YYYYMMDD-001.md</span>

<span class="cmt"># Equivalent report path</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --incident gate-inc-YYYYMMDD-001</span></div>
<p>The JSON record is read from <code>~/.gate/incidents</code>; the Markdown report is written to the current working directory. The report always labels severity “High” and reflects the stored action fields. It does not verify that any step happened and is not a formal incident-management system.</p>

<h2 id="purge">Purge assets contain the secret</h2>
<p><code>git-filter-repo</code> needs the literal value, so the generated replacement file contains plaintext secrets. The generated script also performs destructive local history cleanup when run. Gate requests owner-only permissions, leaves force-push commands commented, and never runs the script automatically.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Revoke/rotate the credential before history work.</li>
  <li>Back up what you need and coordinate with every collaborator.</li>
  <li>Read both generated files.</li>
  <li>Run only from the intended clean repository.</li>
  <li>Verify the rewritten history independently.</li>
  <li>Delete the plaintext replacement file and handle backups/caches.</li>
  <li>Choose an appropriate protected force-push process; collaborators generally need to re-clone.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="minimum">Minimum defensible response</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Revoke and replace the credential.</li>
  <li>Review provider logs for the full plausible exposure period.</li>
  <li>Remove it from current source and deployment configuration.</li>
  <li>Decide whether history rewriting reduces risk in your environment.</li>
  <li>Search forks, artifacts, CI logs, caches, tickets, and backups.</li>
  <li>Record evidence and obtain human review.</li>
</ul>
